:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5905"] :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda}_1=-2-3\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =2(-2+3\\text{i}+(-2-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2(-4) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
